WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.850
Nastenka's History Half my story you know already,

2
00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:07.970
that is, you know I have an old

3
00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:08.510
grandmother.

4
00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:11.270
If the other half is as brief as

5
00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:14.990
that, I interrupted laughing, be quiet and listen.

6
00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:17.470
First of all, you must promise not to

7
00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:19.570
interrupt me, or else I might lose track

8
00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:21.470
of what I'm saying and end up all

9
00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:23.990
muddled, so listen quietly, will you?

10
00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:26.310
I have an old grandmother.

11
00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:28.730
She's been my guardian ever since I was

12
00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:30.990
a little girl, because my father and mother

13
00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:33.150
both passed away when I was very young.

14
00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:36.730
Grandmother must have been better off financially in

15
00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:39.330
the past, for she often recalls the good

16
00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:39.910
old days.

17
00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:43.030
She's fond of talking about how things were

18
00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:45.430
when she was younger, about the elegance of

19
00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:48.990
society, the way people behaved, and how different

20
00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:50.070
everything is now.

21
00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:53.190
She taught me French herself at first, then

22
00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:54.730
hired a teacher for me when I got

23
00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:55.350
a little older.

24
00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:58.350
When I was fifteen, and now I'm seventeen,

25
00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:00.970
so it wasn't so long ago, we stopped

26
00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:02.130
the lessons altogether.

27
00:01:03.069 --> 00:01:04.830
That was around the time when I began

28
00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:05.810
getting into mischief.

29
00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:08.430
I won't tell you exactly what I did,

30
00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:11.690
because really it wasn't anything serious, but it

31
00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:13.150
was enough to upset grandmother.

32
00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:16.010
One morning she called me to her side

33
00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:18.090
and said that because she was blind and

34
00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:19.910
couldn't keep an eye on me, she had

35
00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:21.170
to take drastic measures.

36
00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:24.010
She took a pin, yes, a real pin,

37
00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:26.470
and fastened my dress to hers.

38
00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:29.050
Then she declared that we would sit like

39
00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:30.810
that for the rest of our lives unless

40
00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:32.130
I started behaving better.

41
00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:33.570
Can you imagine?

42
00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:37.630
Sitting there, tied to her, day after day?

43
00:01:39.190 --> 00:01:40.850
At first there was no escaping her.

44
00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:43.650
I had to work, read, and study right

45
00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:44.250
beside her.

46
00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:46.490
I wasn't even allowed to take a step

47
00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:47.750
away without her permission.

48
00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.290
I did try to outsmart her once.

49
00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:54.170
I persuaded Fekla, our charwoman, to sit in

50
00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:56.050
my place while grandmother was napping.

51
00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:59.550
Fekla is deaf and doesn't hear much of

52
00:01:59.550 --> 00:01:59.910
anything.

53
00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:03.050
Grandmother was dozing in her armchair, so I

54
00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:04.270
thought everything would be fine.

55
00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:07.009
I slipped away to visit a friend who

56
00:02:07.009 --> 00:02:09.490
lived nearby, thinking I'd be back before she

57
00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:10.150
even noticed.

58
00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:13.130
But of course it didn't go as planned.

59
00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:15.810
Grandmother woke up while I was gone and

60
00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:17.030
started asking questions.

61
00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:19.690
She thought I was still sitting there quietly

62
00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:20.450
next to her.

63
00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:22.930
Poor Fekla didn't know what to do.

64
00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:25.870
She couldn't understand what grandmother was saying and

65
00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:26.310
panicked.

66
00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:29.850
In her confusion, she undid the pin and

67
00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:30.490
ran off.

68
00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:34.630
At this point, Nastenka burst out laughing, the

69
00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:36.210
memory clearly amusing her.

70
00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:38.830
Her laughter was infectious and I couldn't help

71
00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:41.070
but laugh with her, but she stopped almost

72
00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:43.290
immediately and gave me a warning look.

73
00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:44.490
I'm serious.

74
00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:46.450
Don't you dare laugh at grandmother.

75
00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:50.030
I'm only laughing because it was funny and—well,

76
00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:50.810
what can I do?

77
00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:53.210
That's just how grandmother is.

78
00:02:53.710 --> 00:02:55.910
But I'm fond of her in my own

79
00:02:55.910 --> 00:02:56.230
way.

80
00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:59.010
Oh, how I caught it that time, though.

81
00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:01.130
As soon as I came back, she pinned

82
00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:03.490
me right back in place, and after that

83
00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:04.990
there was no sneaking off again.

84
00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:07.270
I wasn't allowed to stir an inch.

85
00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:09.650
Oh, I almost forgot to tell you.

86
00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:12.630
Our house belongs to us, or, rather, it

87
00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:13.550
belongs to grandmother.

88
00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:16.870
It's a small wooden house with three windows,

89
00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:18.770
as old as grandmother herself.

90
00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:21.450
There's also a tiny upper story.

91
00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:24.270
Recently a new lodger moved into the upstairs

92
00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:24.690
rooms.

93
00:03:25.630 --> 00:03:27.530
Then you must have had an old lodger

94
00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:29.250
before, I commented casually.

95
00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:32.930
Of course we did, Nastenka replied with a

96
00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:35.650
hint of exasperation, and let me tell you,

97
00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:37.710
he knew how to keep quiet better than

98
00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:38.110
you do.

99
00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:40.450
In fact, he hardly spoke at all.

100
00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:42.950
He was a frail, dried-up little old

101
00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:45.910
man, deaf, blind, and lame.

102
00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:48.770
He was so feeble that eventually he just

103
00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:50.250
couldn't go on living anymore.

104
00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:53.090
He passed away, and after that we had

105
00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:54.990
to find someone new to rent the room.

106
00:03:55.690 --> 00:03:57.330
We can't manage without a lodger.

107
00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:00.230
The rent, along with grandmother's pension, is nearly

108
00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:01.250
all we have to live on.

109
00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:04.830
But this time the new lodger wasn't an

110
00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:05.510
old man.

111
00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:08.790
He was young, a stranger, not from around

112
00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:09.130
here.

113
00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:12.150
Grandmother took him in because he didn't haggle

114
00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:12.770
over the rent.

115
00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:15.430
It was only after he moved in that

116
00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:19.029
she asked me, Nastenka, tell me, what's this

117
00:04:19.029 --> 00:04:20.170
lodger of ours like?

118
00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:22.290
Is he young or old?

119
00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:25.230
I didn't want to lie to her, so

120
00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:26.130
I told her the truth.

121
00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:30.110
Well, grandmother, I said, he isn't exactly young,

122
00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:31.950
but he's not old either.

123
00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:35.310
And is he pleasant-looking, she asked?

124
00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:37.910
I didn't see any point in lying about

125
00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:38.510
that either.

126
00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:42.370
Yes, grandmother, I said, he's pleasant-looking.

127
00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:46.310
Oh, what a nuisance, grandmother exclaimed.

128
00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:47.570
What a nuisance!

129
00:04:48.350 --> 00:04:50.850
I tell you, Nastenka, you'd better not get

130
00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:52.090
any ideas about him.

131
00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:54.050
Times are so different now.

132
00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:56.110
Why even a lodger like this?

133
00:04:56.530 --> 00:04:58.530
And he has to be pleasant-looking, too.

134
00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:00.830
It wasn't like this in the old days.

135
00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:04.670
Grandmother was always reminiscing about the old days,

136
00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:06.890
how everything was better back then.

137
00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:09.530
In the old days, she would say, she

138
00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:11.650
was young and full of energy, the sun

139
00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:15.130
was warmer, the flowers bloomed more brightly, and

140
00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:17.510
even the cream didn't turn sour so quickly.

141
00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:20.850
Everything, it seemed, was perfect in the old

142
00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:21.190
days.

143
00:05:21.970 --> 00:05:24.830
Whenever she started talking like this, I would

144
00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:29.070
sit quietly, holding my tongue, listening patiently while

145
00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:31.570
she lamented the present and glorified the past.

146
00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:34.090
But in my mind I couldn't help but

147
00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:36.010
wonder why she had even brought up the

148
00:05:36.010 --> 00:05:38.290
lodger's looks in the first place.

149
00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:40.650
Why did she suggest it to me?

150
00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:43.230
Why did she ask whether he was young

151
00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:44.270
and good-looking?

152
00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:46.690
But that was all I did.

153
00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:49.970
I wondered for a moment, then I put

154
00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:51.210
the thought out of my mind.

155
00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:54.470
I counted the stitches on my knitting, focused

156
00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:56.990
on my work, and forgot about the lodger

157
00:05:56.990 --> 00:05:57.610
entirely.

158
00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:00.870
Life carried on as usual.

159
00:06:02.390 --> 00:06:04.910
One morning, however, the lodger came into our

160
00:06:04.910 --> 00:06:05.230
room.

161
00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:07.790
He had come to ask Grandmother about her

162
00:06:07.790 --> 00:06:09.530
promise to have his rooms repapered.

163
00:06:10.090 --> 00:06:12.990
The conversation started about wallpaper, but soon drifted

164
00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:13.790
to other topics.

165
00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:18.230
Grandmother, being naturally talkative, kept the conversation going.

166
00:06:18.230 --> 00:06:21.430
At one point she said, "Nastenka, go into

167
00:06:21.430 --> 00:06:24.150
my bedroom and bring me my reckoner." I

168
00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:26.530
jumped up immediately, eager to fetch it for

169
00:06:26.530 --> 00:06:28.850
her, but in my hurry I completely forgot

170
00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:30.830
that my dress was still pinned to hers.

171
00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:35.050
I blushed furiously, not knowing why, and instead

172
00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:37.390
of quietly undoing the pin so that the

173
00:06:37.390 --> 00:06:40.170
lodger wouldn't notice, I leapt up so quickly

174
00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:41.710
that Grandmother's chair shifted.

175
00:06:42.550 --> 00:06:45.530
The lodger's eyes widened slightly, and in that

176
00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:47.930
instant he realized the full extent of my

177
00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:48.370
predicament.

178
00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:51.370
When it dawned on me that he now

179
00:06:51.370 --> 00:06:55.310
knew everything, that he understood the ridiculous situation

180
00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:57.170
I was in, I froze.

181
00:06:58.010 --> 00:07:01.250
I stood as still as a statue, unable

182
00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:03.970
to move, my face burning with embarrassment.

183
00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:08.230
And then, to my utter horror, I felt

184
00:07:08.230 --> 00:07:10.050
tears welling up in my eyes.

185
00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:13.070
I didn't know where to look, didn't know

186
00:07:13.070 --> 00:07:13.770
what to do.

187
00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:17.290
Grandmother, unaware of what was happening, called out

188
00:07:17.290 --> 00:07:20.330
again, "What are you waiting for?" But

189
00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:22.130
her words only made things worse.

190
00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:25.370
The tears began falling freely, and I was

191
00:07:25.370 --> 00:07:27.670
completely overcome with shame and misery.

192
00:07:28.330 --> 00:07:30.170
I felt as though I would sink into

193
00:07:30.170 --> 00:07:31.430
the floor if I could.

194
00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:35.830
The lodger, seeing how distraught I was, must

195
00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:37.030
have felt sorry for me.

196
00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:40.050
Without a word he gave a polite bow

197
00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:41.750
and left the room quietly.

198
00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:45.070
His kindness only deepened my embarrassment.

199
00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:48.530
After that, I became terrified of hearing the

200
00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:49.850
slightest sound in the hallway.

201
00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:52.730
Every creak or shuffle of footsteps made my

202
00:07:52.730 --> 00:07:55.950
heart race, and I would immediately think, "It's

203
00:07:55.950 --> 00:07:59.510
him, the lodger!" I would frantically undo

204
00:07:59.510 --> 00:08:01.310
the pin, just in case I needed to

205
00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:03.830
escape quickly, but it never was him.

206
00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:07.710
Day after day passed, and he didn't come

207
00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:08.110
back.

208
00:08:08.110 --> 00:08:11.070
A whole fortnight went by without any sign

209
00:08:11.070 --> 00:08:11.390
of him.

210
00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:14.750
Then one day he sent a message through

211
00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:15.150
Fekla.

212
00:08:15.790 --> 00:08:17.590
He told her he had a large collection

213
00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:21.610
of French books, good, worthwhile books, and that

214
00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:23.290
I might like to borrow them so I

215
00:08:23.290 --> 00:08:24.070
wouldn't get bored.

216
00:08:24.850 --> 00:08:27.570
He added that Grandmother might approve of this,

217
00:08:28.010 --> 00:08:29.530
and he hoped it would help pass the

218
00:08:29.530 --> 00:08:29.910
time.

219
00:08:30.830 --> 00:08:33.730
When Fekla delivered the message, Grandmother, as always,

220
00:08:33.929 --> 00:08:34.549
was cautious.

221
00:08:34.549 --> 00:08:37.909
She accepted his kind offer with gratitude, but

222
00:08:37.909 --> 00:08:39.710
immediately began asking questions.

223
00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:42.510
"Are the books moral?" she asked.

224
00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:45.510
"Because if they're not moral, then it's out

225
00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:46.150
of the question.

226
00:08:46.690 --> 00:08:49.330
You mustn't read anything improper, Nastenka.

227
00:08:49.830 --> 00:08:53.870
People learn terrible things from such books." "What

228
00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:56.150
terrible things, Grandmother?" I asked.

229
00:08:56.570 --> 00:08:59.910
"What could possibly be in them?" "Oh,

230
00:08:59.950 --> 00:09:02.090
plenty," Grandmother said gravely.

231
00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:05.090
"These books are full of stories about how

232
00:09:05.090 --> 00:09:07.570
young men seduce innocent, virtuous girls.

233
00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:10.870
They pretend to love them, promise to marry

234
00:09:10.870 --> 00:09:13.130
them, and then whisk them away from their

235
00:09:13.130 --> 00:09:15.810
parents' homes, but once they've ruined the poor

236
00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:19.390
girls' reputations, they abandon them, leaving them to

237
00:09:19.390 --> 00:09:22.370
suffer the most pitiful fates." "I've read many

238
00:09:22.370 --> 00:09:25.130
books like that myself," she added, almost

239
00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:28.230
as an afterthought, "and I must say, they're

240
00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:30.390
written so vividly that you can't help but

241
00:09:30.390 --> 00:09:31.830
stay up all night reading them.

242
00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:34.370
Of course, I read them on the sly

243
00:09:34.370 --> 00:09:36.850
," she admitted with a sly little smile.

244
00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:41.070
"But you, Nastenka, mustn't read such things.

245
00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:44.450
What books has he sent you, anyway?"

246
00:09:44.450 --> 00:09:46.930
"They're all Walter Scott novels, Grandmother," I

247
00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:47.330
replied.

248
00:09:48.350 --> 00:09:51.430
"Walter Scott's novels?" she repeated thoughtfully.

249
00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:53.630
"Well, those sound proper enough.

250
00:09:54.030 --> 00:09:56.150
But wait, are you sure he hasn't hidden

251
00:09:56.150 --> 00:09:57.130
anything inside them?

252
00:09:57.510 --> 00:09:59.170
These young men can be crafty.

253
00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:01.090
Have you checked to see if there's a

254
00:10:01.090 --> 00:10:04.390
love letter tucked inside the pages?" "No,

255
00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:07.310
Grandmother, there's no love letter," I assured

256
00:10:07.310 --> 00:10:07.550
her.

257
00:10:08.230 --> 00:10:11.370
"Hmm," she said, unconvinced, "have you looked

258
00:10:11.370 --> 00:10:12.030
under the binding?

259
00:10:12.450 --> 00:10:14.970
Sometimes they slip things under the binding, those

260
00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:19.090
rascals." "No, Grandmother," I said again, "there's

261
00:10:19.090 --> 00:10:22.130
nothing under the binding." "Well, then," she

262
00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:26.210
finally conceded, "if you're sure, that's all right."

263
00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:29.250
So we began reading Walter Scott, and within

264
00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:30.990
a month or so we had made our

265
00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:33.110
way through almost half of the novels he'd

266
00:10:33.110 --> 00:10:33.530
sent us.

267
00:10:34.330 --> 00:10:35.970
Then more books began to arrive.

268
00:10:36.610 --> 00:10:38.770
He sent us Pushkin as well, and soon

269
00:10:38.770 --> 00:10:41.130
enough I couldn't imagine my days without a

270
00:10:41.130 --> 00:10:41.370
book.

271
00:10:42.390 --> 00:10:44.990
I left behind my childish daydreams of marrying

272
00:10:44.990 --> 00:10:47.850
a Chinese prince, for now the stories I

273
00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:50.050
read filled my mind with far richer and

274
00:10:50.050 --> 00:10:51.370
more vivid fantasies.

275
00:10:52.210 --> 00:10:55.110
That's how things were when one day, quite

276
00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:57.730
by chance, I encountered our lodger on the

277
00:10:57.730 --> 00:10:58.130
stairs.

278
00:10:58.970 --> 00:11:01.010
Grandmother had sent me to fetch something, and

279
00:11:01.010 --> 00:11:02.770
I was hurrying down when I saw him

280
00:11:02.770 --> 00:11:03.330
coming up.

281
00:11:04.150 --> 00:11:06.410
As we passed, he stopped, and for a

282
00:11:06.410 --> 00:11:07.710
moment neither of us spoke.

283
00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:10.410
I blushed, he blushed.

284
00:11:11.130 --> 00:11:12.890
Then he broke the silence with a friendly

285
00:11:12.890 --> 00:11:15.670
laugh, greeted me with a cheerful, "Good morning

286
00:11:15.670 --> 00:11:17.990
," and asked how Grandmother was doing.

287
00:11:18.850 --> 00:11:21.330
"Well," he added, "have you been reading

288
00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:23.910
the books I sent?" I nodded and

289
00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:24.730
said that I had.

290
00:11:25.170 --> 00:11:27.170
"Which ones did you like best?" he

291
00:11:27.170 --> 00:11:27.470
asked.

292
00:11:28.630 --> 00:11:31.870
"Ivanhoe," I answered, "and Pushkin, most of

293
00:11:31.870 --> 00:11:35.210
all." That was the extent of our conversation

294
00:11:35.210 --> 00:11:35.850
that time.

295
00:11:36.250 --> 00:11:38.730
I hurried away, feeling both shy and strangely

296
00:11:38.730 --> 00:11:41.410
pleased, while he continued on his way upstairs.

297
00:11:42.110 --> 00:11:44.530
A week later I ran into him again,

298
00:11:44.770 --> 00:11:46.570
this time in much the same place.

299
00:11:46.570 --> 00:11:49.550
Only this time Grandmother hadn't sent me on

300
00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:49.950
an errand.

301
00:11:50.490 --> 00:11:52.470
I had gone downstairs of my own accord

302
00:11:52.470 --> 00:11:53.310
to fetch something.

303
00:11:54.050 --> 00:11:56.570
It was just past two, and I knew

304
00:11:56.570 --> 00:11:58.650
from habit that this was the hour he

305
00:11:58.650 --> 00:11:59.870
usually returned home.

306
00:12:00.850 --> 00:12:03.230
"Good afternoon," he said, as he stopped

307
00:12:03.230 --> 00:12:03.850
on the stairs.

308
00:12:04.550 --> 00:12:07.210
"Good afternoon," I replied, trying to keep

309
00:12:07.210 --> 00:12:08.010
my voice steady.

310
00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:11.910
"Aren't you bored?" he asked, "sitting with

311
00:12:11.910 --> 00:12:14.830
your Grandmother all day long?" When he

312
00:12:14.830 --> 00:12:17.550
said this, I felt an immediate rush of

313
00:12:17.550 --> 00:12:18.050
embarrassment.

314
00:12:18.770 --> 00:12:19.650
I didn't know why.

315
00:12:20.510 --> 00:12:22.150
Perhaps it was because I felt as though

316
00:12:22.150 --> 00:12:24.650
he had touched on a sensitive subject, one

317
00:12:24.650 --> 00:12:26.390
that others had already whispered about.

318
00:12:26.930 --> 00:12:28.790
It wasn't the first time someone had hinted

319
00:12:28.790 --> 00:12:31.450
that my life was dull or limited, and

320
00:12:31.450 --> 00:12:33.530
though I didn't like hearing it, I couldn't

321
00:12:33.530 --> 00:12:35.210
deny there was truth in the question.

322
00:12:35.750 --> 00:12:39.130
I felt both ashamed and offended, but I

323
00:12:39.130 --> 00:12:41.850
also found myself rooted to the spot, unable

324
00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:42.730
to walk away.

325
00:12:43.890 --> 00:12:46.830
"Listen," he said gently, "you're a good

326
00:12:46.830 --> 00:12:47.150
girl.

327
00:12:47.870 --> 00:12:50.350
Forgive me for speaking so directly, but I

328
00:12:50.350 --> 00:12:53.430
truly want what's best for you, just as

329
00:12:53.430 --> 00:12:54.690
much as your Grandmother does.

330
00:12:55.270 --> 00:12:57.210
Don't you have any friends you could visit,

331
00:12:57.650 --> 00:12:59.650
someone to talk to or spend time with

332
00:12:59.650 --> 00:13:01.490
?" I told him the truth.

333
00:13:01.950 --> 00:13:04.050
"I don't have any friends now," I

334
00:13:04.050 --> 00:13:04.270
said.

335
00:13:04.530 --> 00:13:07.090
"I used to have one, Mashenka, but she

336
00:13:07.090 --> 00:13:10.450
moved away to Pskov." "I see," he

337
00:13:10.450 --> 00:13:11.070
said thoughtfully.

338
00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:14.510
Then, after a pause, he added, "Would you

339
00:13:14.510 --> 00:13:15.730
like to go to the theatre with me

340
00:13:15.730 --> 00:13:19.690
sometime?" "The theatre?" I repeated, startled.

341
00:13:19.890 --> 00:13:23.070
"But what about Grandmother?" "You'd have to

342
00:13:23.070 --> 00:13:24.990
go without telling her," he said with

343
00:13:24.990 --> 00:13:25.810
a quiet smile.

344
00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:29.410
"No," I said firmly, "I don't want

345
00:13:29.410 --> 00:13:30.410
to deceive Grandmother.

346
00:13:30.910 --> 00:13:34.530
Good-bye." "Good-bye," he replied, and

347
00:13:34.530 --> 00:13:36.590
though he said nothing more, there was something

348
00:13:36.590 --> 00:13:39.010
in his voice that lingered with me, even

349
00:13:39.010 --> 00:13:40.010
after he was gone.

350
00:13:41.250 --> 00:13:43.590
Later that day, after dinner, he came to

351
00:13:43.590 --> 00:13:45.010
see us in our little sitting room.

352
00:13:45.710 --> 00:13:47.510
He stayed for quite a while talking to

353
00:13:47.510 --> 00:13:50.490
Grandmother about this and that, asking her questions

354
00:13:50.490 --> 00:13:51.810
about her life and habits.

355
00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:54.670
At one point he casually asked if she

356
00:13:54.670 --> 00:13:57.210
ever went out, if she had acquaintances or

357
00:13:57.210 --> 00:13:58.410
places she liked to visit.

358
00:13:59.510 --> 00:14:02.270
Grandmother shook her head, laughing, and said she

359
00:14:02.270 --> 00:14:04.110
was too old for such things now.

360
00:14:04.830 --> 00:14:08.070
Then, quite suddenly, he said, "I've taken a

361
00:14:08.070 --> 00:14:09.430
box at the opera for tonight.

362
00:14:09.850 --> 00:14:11.470
They're performing The Barber of Seville.

363
00:14:11.790 --> 00:14:13.890
My friends were supposed to join me, but

364
00:14:13.890 --> 00:14:16.130
they've cancelled, and now the ticket is going

365
00:14:16.130 --> 00:14:19.690
to waste." "The Barber of Seville!" Grandmother

366
00:14:19.690 --> 00:14:21.970
exclaimed, her face lighting up with interest.

367
00:14:22.370 --> 00:14:24.410
"Why, that's the very same one they performed

368
00:14:24.410 --> 00:14:27.690
in the old days." "Yes," he said

369
00:14:27.690 --> 00:14:29.870
with a smile, glancing briefly at me.

370
00:14:30.530 --> 00:14:33.950
"It's the same barber." I could feel my

371
00:14:33.950 --> 00:14:34.650
face burning.

372
00:14:35.110 --> 00:14:36.890
I knew what he meant by that glance,

373
00:14:37.090 --> 00:14:39.690
and my heart began pounding in suspense.

374
00:14:40.610 --> 00:14:42.390
I kept my eyes on the floor, not

375
00:14:42.390 --> 00:14:43.370
daring to look up.

376
00:14:44.010 --> 00:14:46.630
But I could feel Grandmother's excitement growing.

377
00:14:47.050 --> 00:14:48.990
"To think of it," she said, "I

378
00:14:48.990 --> 00:14:51.450
once played Rosina myself in a private performance.

379
00:14:51.710 --> 00:14:55.430
That was many, many years ago." "Then perhaps

380
00:14:55.430 --> 00:14:57.010
you'd like to go to-night," the

381
00:14:57.010 --> 00:14:57.810
lodger suggested.

382
00:14:58.210 --> 00:14:59.850
"It would be a shame for the ticket

383
00:14:59.850 --> 00:15:03.550
to go unused." "By all means, let's go

384
00:15:03.550 --> 00:15:05.470
," Grandmother declared with enthusiasm.

385
00:15:06.070 --> 00:15:06.910
"Why shouldn't we?

386
00:15:07.390 --> 00:15:10.570
And besides, my Nastenka here has never been

387
00:15:10.570 --> 00:15:13.690
to the theatre in her life." My goodness,

388
00:15:13.950 --> 00:15:14.610
what joy!

389
00:15:15.230 --> 00:15:17.530
As soon as the plan was decided, we

390
00:15:17.530 --> 00:15:19.650
began getting ready, without delay.

391
00:15:20.390 --> 00:15:22.430
Grandmother and I put on our very best

392
00:15:22.430 --> 00:15:22.950
clothes.

393
00:15:23.590 --> 00:15:26.050
Even though she couldn't see, she was eager

394
00:15:26.050 --> 00:15:28.250
to hear the music, and I could tell

395
00:15:28.250 --> 00:15:30.290
she was just as excited as I was,

396
00:15:30.670 --> 00:15:31.830
though for a different reason.

397
00:15:32.730 --> 00:15:35.270
She was a kind and selfless soul, my

398
00:15:35.270 --> 00:15:38.450
Grandmother, and what mattered most to her was

399
00:15:38.450 --> 00:15:40.330
that I had the chance to enjoy myself.

400
00:15:40.950 --> 00:15:42.650
She would never have gone to the theatre

401
00:15:42.650 --> 00:15:44.750
on her own, but knowing it would bring

402
00:15:44.750 --> 00:15:46.950
me happiness she agreed without hesitation.

403
00:15:47.970 --> 00:15:50.430
As for me, I could hardly contain my

404
00:15:50.430 --> 00:15:50.890
excitement.

405
00:15:51.570 --> 00:15:53.450
The anticipation alone was thrilling.

406
00:15:53.450 --> 00:15:56.450
When we finally arrived at the theatre and

407
00:15:56.450 --> 00:15:58.970
took our seats, I could scarcely focus on

408
00:15:58.970 --> 00:16:00.130
anything around me.

409
00:16:00.710 --> 00:16:04.410
The lights, the crowd, the atmosphere, it was

410
00:16:04.410 --> 00:16:06.550
all so overwhelming and magical.

411
00:16:07.610 --> 00:16:09.710
What my impressions of the Barbara of Seville

412
00:16:09.710 --> 00:16:11.990
were, I won't even attempt to describe.

413
00:16:12.530 --> 00:16:15.990
The music, the performance, the characters, it felt

414
00:16:15.990 --> 00:16:18.050
like I had stepped into an entirely new

415
00:16:18.050 --> 00:16:18.470
world.

416
00:16:18.470 --> 00:16:21.030
But what stood out even more to me

417
00:16:21.030 --> 00:16:23.650
that evening was the way our lodger behaved.

418
00:16:24.390 --> 00:16:26.450
He looked at me so kindly, spoke so

419
00:16:26.450 --> 00:16:29.090
warmly, that it was impossible to mistake his

420
00:16:29.090 --> 00:16:29.590
intentions.

421
00:16:30.330 --> 00:16:32.710
I realized then that his suggestion that morning,

422
00:16:33.050 --> 00:16:34.370
that I should go to the theatre with

423
00:16:34.370 --> 00:16:36.790
him alone, had been a test, a way

424
00:16:36.790 --> 00:16:39.510
of gauging my character, and knowing that made

425
00:16:39.510 --> 00:16:41.970
me feel proud, like I had passed some

426
00:16:41.970 --> 00:16:43.090
unspoken challenge.

427
00:16:43.950 --> 00:16:46.110
That night, as I lay in bed, I

428
00:16:46.110 --> 00:16:47.410
was brimming with happiness.

429
00:16:47.410 --> 00:16:50.350
My heart was beating so fast that I

430
00:16:50.350 --> 00:16:51.430
thought I might have a fever.

431
00:16:52.070 --> 00:16:55.050
I couldn't stop thinking about the performance, replaying

432
00:16:55.050 --> 00:16:56.810
every moment of it in my mind.

433
00:16:57.630 --> 00:16:59.730
All night long I dreamed of the Barbara

434
00:16:59.730 --> 00:17:02.110
of Seville, and when I woke the next

435
00:17:02.110 --> 00:17:04.270
morning it felt as though I were still

436
00:17:04.270 --> 00:17:05.130
under its spell.

437
00:17:06.470 --> 00:17:09.410
Naturally, I expected that after such a delightful

438
00:17:09.410 --> 00:17:12.210
evening, the lodger would start visiting us more

439
00:17:12.210 --> 00:17:12.690
often.

440
00:17:12.690 --> 00:17:16.410
But to my disappointment, that wasn't what happened

441
00:17:16.410 --> 00:17:16.910
at all.

442
00:17:17.630 --> 00:17:19.710
He seemed to grow even more distant.

443
00:17:20.490 --> 00:17:22.490
He only came by about once a month,

444
00:17:22.550 --> 00:17:24.550
and even then it was only to invite

445
00:17:24.550 --> 00:17:25.730
us to the theatre again.

446
00:17:26.250 --> 00:17:28.990
We went twice more, but by then my

447
00:17:28.990 --> 00:17:30.490
joy had started to wane.

448
00:17:31.230 --> 00:17:33.070
I began to feel that he didn't truly

449
00:17:33.070 --> 00:17:35.690
care for me, that his kindness was only

450
00:17:35.690 --> 00:17:37.210
out of pity for my situation.

451
00:17:37.210 --> 00:17:40.670
He saw how strictly Grandmother treated me, and

452
00:17:40.670 --> 00:17:42.970
that was the extent of his feelings, sympathy,

453
00:17:43.450 --> 00:17:43.930
nothing more.

454
00:17:45.550 --> 00:17:47.690
As time passed, I grew restless.

455
00:17:48.290 --> 00:17:51.150
I couldn't focus on anything, not my reading,

456
00:17:51.570 --> 00:17:54.350
not my knitting, not even my conversations with

457
00:17:54.350 --> 00:17:54.850
Grandmother.

458
00:17:55.650 --> 00:17:59.170
Some days I found myself laughing uncontrollably, doing

459
00:17:59.170 --> 00:18:01.490
silly things just to provoke a reaction from

460
00:18:01.490 --> 00:18:01.670
her.

461
00:18:02.510 --> 00:18:04.470
Other days I would break down in tears

462
00:18:04.470 --> 00:18:05.630
for no apparent reason.

463
00:18:05.630 --> 00:18:08.690
My emotions were all over the place, and

464
00:18:08.690 --> 00:18:09.870
I couldn't make sense of them.

465
00:18:10.430 --> 00:18:12.730
I began to lose weight and felt constantly

466
00:18:12.730 --> 00:18:15.310
on edge, as though something inside me was

467
00:18:15.310 --> 00:18:16.050
about to break.

468
00:18:16.770 --> 00:18:19.610
When the opera season ended, the lodger stopped

469
00:18:19.610 --> 00:18:20.990
coming to see us entirely.

470
00:18:21.730 --> 00:18:23.570
The only times I saw him were by

471
00:18:23.570 --> 00:18:24.950
chance, on the stairs.

472
00:18:25.730 --> 00:18:29.510
Each time he would bow silently, gravely, as

473
00:18:29.510 --> 00:18:31.950
if trying to avoid any unnecessary interaction.

474
00:18:31.950 --> 00:18:35.010
He would continue on his way, and I

475
00:18:35.010 --> 00:18:37.330
would stand there, rooted to the spot, my

476
00:18:37.330 --> 00:18:40.810
face burning red with embarrassment, my heart pounding

477
00:18:40.810 --> 00:18:42.030
as though it might burst.

478
00:18:42.950 --> 00:18:44.350
Then came the end.

479
00:18:44.890 --> 00:18:47.370
It was exactly a year ago, in May,

480
00:18:47.710 --> 00:18:49.150
when the lodger came to us with the

481
00:18:49.150 --> 00:18:49.490
news.

482
00:18:50.050 --> 00:18:52.290
He told Grandmother that his business in Petersburg

483
00:18:52.290 --> 00:18:54.430
was finished, and that he would be returning

484
00:18:54.430 --> 00:18:55.770
to Moscow for a year.

485
00:18:56.410 --> 00:18:58.650
As soon as I heard those words, I

486
00:18:58.650 --> 00:19:00.530
felt the ground shift beneath me.

487
00:19:00.530 --> 00:19:03.670
My legs gave out, and I sank into

488
00:19:03.670 --> 00:19:05.870
a chair, barely able to breathe.

489
00:19:06.890 --> 00:19:08.450
Grandmother of course noticed nothing.

490
00:19:09.050 --> 00:19:11.430
She simply listened politely as he explained his

491
00:19:11.430 --> 00:19:13.750
plans, and when he finished, he bowed and

492
00:19:13.750 --> 00:19:14.390
left the room.

493
00:19:15.030 --> 00:19:16.270
I was devastated.

494
00:19:16.850 --> 00:19:17.810
What was I to do?

495
00:19:18.390 --> 00:19:21.250
I thought and thought, turning everything over in

496
00:19:21.250 --> 00:19:23.070
my mind, but nothing made sense.

497
00:19:23.670 --> 00:19:26.750
I felt trapped, helpless, as though my world

498
00:19:26.750 --> 00:19:28.090
were collapsing around me.

499
00:19:28.810 --> 00:19:30.930
Finally, I made up my mind.

500
00:19:31.530 --> 00:19:34.110
The next day, he was to leave, and

501
00:19:34.110 --> 00:19:36.370
I resolved to take action that very evening,

502
00:19:36.910 --> 00:19:38.490
after Grandmother had gone to bed.

503
00:19:39.410 --> 00:19:40.510
So it happened.

504
00:19:41.810 --> 00:19:44.870
I gathered all my clothes, packed the linen

505
00:19:44.870 --> 00:19:47.130
I would need, and tied everything into a

506
00:19:47.130 --> 00:19:47.410
bundle.

507
00:19:48.110 --> 00:19:50.270
With the parcel in hand, I climbed the

508
00:19:50.270 --> 00:19:52.590
stairs to his room, more dead than alive.

509
00:19:53.390 --> 00:19:55.970
My heart was pounding so violently that I

510
00:19:55.970 --> 00:19:56.890
thought I might faint.

511
00:19:56.890 --> 00:19:59.330
I stood outside his door for what felt

512
00:19:59.330 --> 00:20:02.010
like an eternity, unable to summon the courage

513
00:20:02.010 --> 00:20:02.590
to knock.

514
00:20:03.390 --> 00:20:05.210
When I finally opened the door and stepped

515
00:20:05.210 --> 00:20:07.070
inside, he turned to look at me.

516
00:20:07.710 --> 00:20:09.790
His face went pale, and he cried out

517
00:20:09.790 --> 00:20:10.350
in shock.

518
00:20:10.750 --> 00:20:11.930
He thought I was a ghost.

519
00:20:12.950 --> 00:20:14.810
Rushing to my side, he fetched a glass

520
00:20:14.810 --> 00:20:16.590
of water, for I could barely stand.

521
00:20:17.250 --> 00:20:19.430
My head was spinning, and I felt as

522
00:20:19.430 --> 00:20:20.970
though I were about to collapse.

523
00:20:20.970 --> 00:20:24.230
When I finally recovered enough to speak, I

524
00:20:24.230 --> 00:20:26.550
placed my parcel on his bed, sat down

525
00:20:26.550 --> 00:20:29.330
beside it, and buried my face in my

526
00:20:29.330 --> 00:20:29.790
hands.

527
00:20:30.770 --> 00:20:33.370
Tears poured from my eyes in a torrent,

528
00:20:33.810 --> 00:20:35.410
and I couldn't stop myself.

529
00:20:36.130 --> 00:20:38.450
I cried as though my heart were breaking,

530
00:20:39.010 --> 00:20:40.270
and in a way it was.

531
00:20:40.930 --> 00:20:43.110
I think he understood everything in that moment.

532
00:20:44.110 --> 00:20:45.590
He didn't need me to explain.

533
00:20:45.590 --> 00:20:48.270
He looked at me with such sadness, such

534
00:20:48.270 --> 00:20:51.750
quiet regret, that it tore at my heart

535
00:20:51.750 --> 00:20:52.410
even more.

536
00:20:53.450 --> 00:20:55.210
Listen, he said gently.

537
00:20:56.090 --> 00:20:57.210
Listen, Nastenka.

538
00:20:57.810 --> 00:20:59.330
I can't do anything.

539
00:20:59.910 --> 00:21:01.170
I am a poor man.

540
00:21:01.610 --> 00:21:04.250
I have nothing, not even a proper job.

541
00:21:04.710 --> 00:21:06.570
How could we live if I were to

542
00:21:06.570 --> 00:21:07.130
marry you?

543
00:21:08.050 --> 00:21:09.850
We talked for a long time.

544
00:21:10.210 --> 00:21:12.470
I poured my heart out, telling him that

545
00:21:12.470 --> 00:21:14.710
I couldn't bear living with Grandmother any longer,

546
00:21:14.710 --> 00:21:17.250
that I felt like I was suffocating, that

547
00:21:17.250 --> 00:21:18.910
I would rather run away than go on

548
00:21:18.910 --> 00:21:19.430
like this.

549
00:21:20.090 --> 00:21:21.570
I even said that I would follow him

550
00:21:21.570 --> 00:21:23.790
to Moscow if only he would let me.

551
00:21:24.850 --> 00:21:27.930
Shame, pride, and love were all swirling within

552
00:21:27.930 --> 00:21:30.290
me, clashing and fighting for control.

553
00:21:31.490 --> 00:21:34.470
At last, I collapsed onto the bed, overcome

554
00:21:34.470 --> 00:21:37.310
